<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Coburg is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-franconia">Upper Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, dominated by the fortress of Veste Coburg, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> stayed in 1530. It was the residence of the Ernestine dukes of Saxe-Coburg and later of Saxe-Coburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, whose family supplied monarchs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. After a referendum in 1920 Coburg joined Bavaria rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coburg.jpg" alt="Coburg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: © Travis K. Witt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marktplatz_Coburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Victoria married her cousin Albert of Saxe-<b>Coburg</b> and Gotha at the Chapel Royal, St James's Palace.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-10">10 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Ernestine redistribution of 1826, Duke Ernest I of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld gave up Saalfeld and received Gotha, forming Saxe-<b>Coburg</b> and Gotha.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Duchy of Saxe-Coburg died out in 1699, he gained most of <b>Coburg</b> after a long dispute, forming Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-coburg-saalfeld">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld</a></li><li><blockquote>On his death in 1675 his lands were divided among seven sons, creating the duchies of <b>Coburg</b>, Meiningen, Römhild, Eisenberg, Hildburghausen and Saalfeld.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Gotha</a></li><li><blockquote>The Erfurt Division of 1572 gave Duke Frederick William I the lands around Weimar, while his cousins received <b>Coburg</b> and Eisenach.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Coburg</b> and Gotha, legally separate since 1826, went their own ways: Gotha joined Thuringia, while Coburg became a free state of its own, with its parliament meeting in Coburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-coburg">Free State of Coburg</a></li></ul></section>
